    {
      "index": 3,
      "id": "motivation",
      "heading": "Motivation",
      "chunks": [
        {
          "chunk_index": 1,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c01",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c1_approximation_theory_offers_two_comp",
          "text": "Approximation theory offers two complementary kinds of statements. Forward, or Jackson-type, theorems tell you how well a model can approximate a sufficiently regular target.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "approximation",
            "theory",
            "offers",
            "two",
            "complementary",
            "kinds",
            "statements",
            "forward",
            "jackson-type",
            "theorems"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 2,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c02",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c2_inverse_bernstein_type_theorems_run",
          "text": "Inverse, or Bernstein-type, theorems run the other direction: they assume a target can be efficiently approximated and then deduce what regularity the target must have.",
          "expected_role": "content",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "inverse",
            "bernstein-type",
            "theorems",
            "run",
            "other",
            "direction",
            "they",
            "assume",
            "target",
            "efficiently"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 3,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c03",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c3_inverse_theorems_precisely_tool_expo",
          "text": "Inverse theorems are precisely the tool for exposing fundamental limitations of an architecture. Earlier work proved such a result for linear RNNs, showing that efficiently approximable linear targets must have exponentially decaying memory.",
          "expected_role": "method",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "inverse",
            "theorems",
            "precisely",
            "tool",
            "exposing",
            "fundamental",
            "limitations",
            "architecture",
            "earlier",
            "work"
          ],
          "required": true
        },
        {
          "chunk_index": 4,
          "chunk_id": "s03_c04",
          "anchor_id": "cue_s03_c4_pressing_question_whether_adding_non",
          "text": "The pressing question was whether adding nonlinearity, which greatly increases model capacity, would break this so-called curse of memory. This paper was motivated to settle that.",
          "expected_role": "title",
          "cue_keywords": [
            "pressing",
            "question",
            "whether",
            "adding",
            "nonlinearity",
            "which",
            "greatly",
            "increases",
            "capacity",
